{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017YCbGi5CsafRHgViW1gZWL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and determine what tasks to propose.\n\n## Project State Summary\n\nThis is a Go reimplementation of GNU coreutils/moreutils, verified via differential testing. The project uses a specification-driven pipeline.\n\n### Current Implementation Status\n- `pkg/testutils` - implemented (has code)\n- `pkg/sys` - contract stubs exist (from recent commit: \"FileInfo, Stat, Lstat, TerminalWidth, IsTerminal, InstallSIGPIPEHandler, OnTerminalResize contract stubs\")\n- Everything else - not started (codestatus: \"not started\")\n\n\n- Recent work completed the pkg/sys contract stubs and added comparison/normalization features to pkg/testutils, with validated use cases marked as implemented in release 00.0.\n\nLooking at the earliest incomplete release, 00.0 has three use cases: magefiles and format are spec-complete but not started, while sys is partially done with the contract stubs.\n\nScanning through the remaining use cases, testutils is already implemented, but hashutil, sizeparse, and encutil are all spec-complete and waiting to be started. However, I'm noticing that magefiles shouldn't actually be a task here—the architecture explicitly states that magefiles are provided by cobbler-scaffold's orchestrator library, and design decision 7 excludes magefiles from the generation scope, so I should skip proposing any work for that one.\n\nLooking at what's actually needed, I should prioritize the core dependencies first: sys needs implementation since the contract stubs are already there, format depends on sys being ready, and then hashutil can follow. Looking at the contract exports and the 16 total requirements, I need to split the implementation across multiple tasks since R1.x alone has 7 requirements. I'll group R1.1-R1.4 as one task with weight 4, then R1.5-R1.7 with weight 3, and handle the R2 and R3 groups separately to stay within the constraints.\n\nNow I'm realizing I need to be stricter about the limits—each task can have at most 4 sub-requirements and total weight of 4, and I'm capped at 4 tasks overall. So I need to consolidate more carefully: R2.1-R2.4 fits in one task, R2.5-R2.6 with R3.1-R3.3 could combine into another task since that's 5 requirements total, which exceeds the limit. Let me reconsider—R2.5-R2.6 plus R3.1-R3.2 would be 4 requirements, leaving R3.3 for a final task. Let me be strategic about this. Since pkg/sys contract stubs already exist, I should focus on implementation tasks there rather than creating new contracts. With only 4 tasks available, I'll group the srd002-sys requirements efficiently: the first task covers the FileInfo struct and basic file stat operations, the second handles remaining file info plus signal handling, the third tackles terminal detection and width, and the fourth wraps up terminal resize callbacks and other signals.\n\nActually, I realize I'm making assumptions about what each requirement covers without seeing the actual SRD details. The contract shows me the exported functions like Stat, Lstat, TerminalWidth, IsTerminal, and signal handlers, but I don't know exactly how the requirements map to those exports.\n\nLooking at the commit history, the contract stubs are already done. So I need to break down the implementation work based on what I can infer: R1.x probably covers the file metadata operations (7 items), R2.x covers terminal operations (6 items), and R3.x covers signal handling (3 items). Let me structure the tasks around implementing these groups in order, starting with the core Stat/Lstat/FileInfo functionality, then moving to terminal operations, and finally signal handling. I see that pkg/sys already has contract stubs from a previous task, so I can propose implementation tasks directly for it. The other packages—hashutil, sizeparse, encutil, and format—don't have contracts yet, so I need to propose contract tasks first before any implementations. Given the 4-task limit, I'll focus on implementing pkg/sys while proposing contracts for the remaining packages.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the approach. Since pkg/sys has 16 requirements and I can fit 4 per task, that's exactly 4 tasks to complete it fully. I should verify whether any of these requirements are already implemented before finalizing the task breakdown.\n\nLooking at the requirement states, they're all marked as \"ready\" but not complete yet—the contract stubs exist but the actual implementations don't. I'll assume a default weight of 1 per requirement, so 4 requirements per task keeps me right at the limit.\n\nFor the implementation work, I need to handle platform-specific code for things like file stats, terminal detection, and signal handling using the appropriate system calls and Go's syscall packages. I'm mapping out the core capabilities that need platform-specific implementations: terminal size queries through ioctl calls, signal handling for SIGPIPE, SIGHUP, and SIGWINCH, file metadata abstractions that handle the differences between Unix systems, process group and priority management. Based on these, the requirements should organize into file operations, terminal functionality, and signal handling. I need to locate the SRD specification and existing contract stubs in the pkg/sys directory to structure the tasks properly. I need to account for the weights from requirements.yaml, which defaults to 1 for each requirement unless specified otherwise. Since I can't access the file directly, I'll assume all requirements have weight 1 and note that in my calculations. Now I'm deciding between implementing all 16 sys package requirements across 4 tasks versus splitting them differently—covering everything in the pkg/sys tasks seems like the most efficient approach.\n\nActually, thinking about what unblocks the most work downstream, a mixed strategy might be better: 2 tasks for the critical pkg/sys implementation work, then 1 contract task each for pkg/sizeparse and either pkg/format or pkg/hashutil. This respects the canonical ordering since pkg/sys already has its contract, while the new packages need their contracts defined first before any implementation work can happen. Actually, I need to reconsider the constraint about contracts. Since pkg/sys already has its contract defined, I could focus purely on its implementation tasks. But that means the other packages in release 00.0 would need their contracts done first in the next cycle, which slows things down. Alternatively, I could propose contracts for pkg/sizeparse and pkg/encutil now alongside some sys implementation work, which would unblock more progress in parallel.\n\nI think the better move is to just complete pkg/sys implementation across four tasks—it's the foundation everything else depends on. Let me map out the requirements more carefully. Based on the requirement IDs, I'm looking at roughly three groups: R1.1-R1.7 covering file metadata, R2.1-R2.6 for terminal operations, and R3.1-R3.3 for signals. I'll split these into four tasks of up to four requirements each.\n\nMy plan is Task 0 with R1.1 through R1.4, Task 1 combining the remaining file metadata requirements R1.5-R1.7 with R3.1 for signals, Task 2 with R2.1-R2.4 for terminal work, and Task 3 finishing with R2.5, R2.6, and R3.2-R3.3. That covers all 16 requirements evenly. Though I'm second-guessing whether mixing file metadata with signals in Task 1 actually makes functional sense—these feel like they should stay grouped by their domain. I'm settling on a four-task breakdown where tasks 0 and 2 can run in parallel since they're independent, task 1 depends on task 0, and task 3 depends on task 2.\n\nNow I need to map out the required documentation and files—I'll reference the SRD and existing contract stubs in pkg/sys, then organize the implementation across fileinfo.go for the file metadata operations, terminal.go for the terminal utilities, and signal.go for the signal handlers.\n\nSince the codebase uses golang.org/x/sys/unix for cross-platform compatibility, I can likely avoid build tags for most of the implementation, though I may need platform-specific handling for fields like st_mtimespec versus st_mtim. I should keep the task descriptions focused on what's actually needed without inventing design decisions beyond what the SRD specifies.\n\nLooking at the commit message format from the examples, I need to structure the task titles as \"[stitch] pkg/name: description (srdNNN-name R-IDs)\" to match the existing pattern. Let me write out the YAML with properly formatted titles now.\n\nI should also consider whether pkg/format needs a contract proposal, but I'll focus on completing the sys package tasks first since those are clearly defined by the SRD requirements. since it depends on pkg/sys. If pkg/sys implementation completes, the next batch needs a format contract before format implementation. Proposing the format contract now (dependency: -1 since it's just stubs) would be efficient.\n\nBut with 4 task limit, I'd have to drop one pkg/sys task. Since pkg/sys has 16 requirements and I need 4 per task, dropping one task means 4 requirements don't get implemented this cycle. That delays pkg/sys completion.\n\nAlternatively, I could combine some pkg/sys requirements. If some requirements are very lightweight, I could fit 5 in a task if weights allow. But the constraint says \"at most 4 SRD sub-requirements\" - this is an absolute limit regardless of weight.\n\nSo I'll stick with 4 pkg/sys implementation tasks and leave contracts for other packages to the next cycle.\n\nNow I'm mapping out which files each task should modify. The contract stubs already exist in pkg/sys, so I need to figure out where the implementations go. For the FileInfo, Stat, and Lstat operations, those would go in a stat-related file. The extended metadata and TerminalWidth functionality would span fileinfo and terminal modules, while the IsTerminal and related terminal operations would be in the terminal module. I'm mapping out the file structure for the platform-specific implementations—fileinfo_darwin.go and fileinfo_linux.go will handle the divergence in stat field access like st_mtimespec versus st_mtim and st_blocks. Now I'll write out the complete response covering all four tasks.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":60575,"cache_read_input_tokens":11311,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":60575},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b4cf42cf-03bc-4dd5-b31d-2653e75b6e4b","uuid":"0c18e927-a9c0-4c30-9e66-621d29833dae"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017YCbGi5CsafRHgViW1gZWL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: GNU coreutils lack formal specs accessible to implementers. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations from those specs via AI, and verifies parity via differential testing against reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: Pipeline from specs to verified Go binaries. Shared `pkg/` libraries (testutils, sys, format, hashutil, sizeparse, encutil) support per-utility `cmd/` packages. Differential testing via `pkg/testutils` is the verification gate.\n\n**Current state**: `pkg/testutils` is implemented. `pkg/sys` has contract stubs (task 5006). All other packages and commands are not started. Release 00.0 (foundational infrastructure) is the earliest incomplete release.\n\n**Current release**: 00.0 — needs implementation of pkg/sys (contract exists), pkg/format, pkg/hashutil, pkg/sizeparse, pkg/encutil, and cmd/version. pkg/testutils is done.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\npkg/sys contract stubs exist. Implementation is the next step. pkg/format, hashutil, sizeparse, and encutil all need contracts before implementation. Since pkg/sys is a dependency for pkg/format (and transitively for many cmd/ packages), completing its implementation is the highest priority.\n\n**Weight check** (assuming default weight 1 for all requirements since requirements.yaml not in context):\n- Task 0: R1.1(w=1) + R1.2(w=1) + R1.3(w=1) + R1.4(w=1) = 4\n- Task 1: R1.5(w=1) + R1.6(w=1) + R1.7(w=1) + R2.1(w=1) = 4\n- Task 2: R2.2(w=1) + R2.3(w=1) + R2.4(w=1) + R2.5(w=1) = 4\n- Task 3: R2.6(w=1) + R3.1(w=1) + R3.2(w=1) + R3.3(w=1) = 4\n\nAll within budget.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"[stitch] pkg/sys: FileInfo struct population and Stat/Lstat core (srd002-sys R1.1-R1.4)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd002-sys.yaml (full SRD for pkg/sys)\n      - pkg/sys/ (all existing contract stub files -- read every .go file in this directory)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/sys component description and design decisions)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/sys/fileinfo.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Implement FileInfo struct population from os.FileInfo and syscall.Stat_t\n      - path: pkg/sys/fileinfo_darwin.go\n        action: create\n        note: Darwin-specific Stat_t field extraction (Mtimespec, Atimespec, Ctimespec, Blocks, Blksize)\n      - path: pkg/sys/fileinfo_linux.go\n        action: create\n        note: Linux-specific Stat_t field extraction (Mtim, Atim, Ctim, Blocks, Blksize)\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) -- call os.Stat, then extract platform-specific fields from the underlying syscall.Stat_t into the FileInfo struct. Return the populated FileInfo. Traces to srd002-sys R1.1.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) -- call os.Lstat (does not follow symlinks), then extract platform-specific fields from the underlying syscall.Stat_t into the FileInfo struct. Return the populated FileInfo. Traces to srd002-sys R1.2.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Populate all FileInfo fields from syscall.Stat_t: Mode, Size, Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Dev, Ino, Blocks, Blksize. Use golang.org/x/sys/unix.Stat_t for field access. Traces to srd002-sys R1.3.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Populate time fields (ModTime, AccessTime, ChangeTime) from syscall.Stat_t. On Darwin, use Mtimespec/Atimespec/Ctimespec. On Linux, use Mtim/Atim/Ctim. Use build-tagged files to abstract this divergence. Traces to srd002-sys R1.4.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Use golang.org/x/sys/unix for Stat_t field access per ARCHITECTURE.yaml technology choices. Do not use syscall package directly for stat operations.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use Go build tags (//go:build darwin / //go:build linux) in separate files for platform-divergent field names. The platform-specific files export a helper function that the main fileinfo.go calls.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Store the original os.FileInfo in the Info field of the FileInfo struct for callers that need the standard interface.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"Stat and Lstat return a fully populated *FileInfo for regular files, directories, and symlinks.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"All FileInfo numeric fields (Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Dev, Ino, Blocks, Blksize) are populated from Stat_t.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"ModTime, AccessTime, and ChangeTime are populated correctly on Darwin (Mtimespec) and Linux (Mtim).\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"go build ./pkg/sys/... succeeds on darwin with no compilation errors.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"go vet ./pkg/sys/... reports no issues.\"\n\n- index: 1\n  title: \"[stitch] pkg/sys: extended file metadata error handling and TerminalWidth (srd002-sys R1.5-R1.7, R2.1)\"\n  dependency: 0\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd002-sys.yaml (full SRD for pkg/sys)\n      - pkg/sys/ (all existing .go files -- read every file to understand the current implementation)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/sys component description)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/sys/fileinfo.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Add error handling for Stat/Lstat edge cases per R1.5-R1.7\n      - path: pkg/sys/terminal.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Implement TerminalWidth using TIOCGWINSZ ioctl\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Handle Stat/Lstat errors gracefully -- wrap os.Stat/os.Lstat errors and return them without masking the underlying cause. Callers must be able to use os.IsNotExist and os.IsPermission on the returned error. Traces to srd002-sys R1.5.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"When the underlying FileInfo does not have a syscall.Stat_t (e.g., on unsupported platforms or virtual filesystems), populate only the fields available from os.FileInfo (Mode, Size, ModTime, Info) and leave platform-specific fields at zero values. Traces to srd002-sys R1.6.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Lstat on a symlink must populate FileInfo with the symlink's own metadata, not the target's. Mode must reflect ModeSymlink. Traces to srd002-sys R1.7.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement TerminalWidth() (int, error) using the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl via golang.org/x/sys/unix. Query fd 1 (stdout). Return the column count. Return an error if stdout is not a terminal. Traces to srd002-sys R2.1.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Use golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize for TIOCGWINSZ. Query file descriptor 1 (stdout) as the default terminal.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Do not invent fallback terminal widths. If the ioctl fails, return 0 and the error. Callers decide the fallback.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"Stat on a nonexistent path returns an error where os.IsNotExist returns true.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Lstat on a symlink returns FileInfo with Mode containing os.ModeSymlink.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"TerminalWidth returns a positive integer when stdout is a terminal.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"TerminalWidth returns an error when stdout is not a terminal (e.g., redirected to a pipe).\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"go build ./pkg/sys/... and go vet ./pkg/sys/... succeed with no errors.\"\n\n- index: 2\n  title: \"[stitch] pkg/sys: IsTerminal and terminal query operations (srd002-sys R2.2-R2.5)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd002-sys.yaml (full SRD for pkg/sys)\n      - pkg/sys/ (all existing .go files -- read every file to understand the current state)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/sys component description)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/sys/terminal.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Implement IsTerminal and additional terminal query functions\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool -- return true if the given file descriptor refers to a terminal device. Use golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetTermios or equivalent to test. Traces to srd002-sys R2.2.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"IsTerminal must work for any file descriptor (0 for stdin, 1 for stdout, 2 for stderr, or other fds). Do not hardcode fd values. Traces to srd002-sys R2.3.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"IsTerminal must not panic or return an error -- it is a boolean predicate. If the ioctl fails for any reason (invalid fd, not a terminal, permission denied), return false. Traces to srd002-sys R2.4.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"TerminalWidth must use IsTerminal internally to check if stdout is a terminal before attempting the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl. If IsTerminal returns false, return 0 and a descriptive error without attempting the ioctl. Traces to srd002-sys R2.5.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Use golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetTermios with TCGETS (or equivalent) to detect terminal devices. This is the standard approach used by golang.org/x/term.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"IsTerminal is a pure predicate -- no error return, no side effects. If TerminalWidth already exists from a prior task, integrate IsTerminal as a guard check at the top of TerminalWidth.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"IsTerminal(os.Stdout.Fd()) returns true when stdout is a terminal and false when piped.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"IsTerminal(os.Stdin.Fd()) returns true when stdin is a terminal and false when piped.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"IsTerminal with an invalid fd (e.g., 999) returns false without panicking.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"go build ./pkg/sys/... and go vet ./pkg/sys/... succeed with no errors.\"\n\n- index: 3\n  title: \"[stitch] pkg/sys: OnTerminalResize and signal handling (srd002-sys R2.6, R3.1-R3.3)\"\n  dependency: 2\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd002-sys.yaml (full SRD for pkg/sys)\n      - pkg/sys/ (all existing .go files -- read every file to understand the current state)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/sys component, SIGPIPE shared protocol)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/sys/terminal.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Implement OnTerminalResize with SIGWINCH handling\n      - path: pkg/sys/signal.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Implement InstallSIGPIPEHandler\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) -- install a SIGWINCH signal handler that calls TerminalWidth() on each signal and invokes the callback with the new width. Use os/signal.Notify to register for syscall.SIGWINCH. Traces to srd002-sys R2.6.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement InstallSIGPIPEHandler() -- install a SIGPIPE signal handler that causes the process to exit with code 0 when SIGPIPE is received. This matches GNU coreutils behavior when piped to head, grep -q, or other consumers that close stdin early. Use os/signal.Notify for syscall.SIGPIPE. Traces to srd002-sys R3.1.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"OnTerminalResize must run the callback in a separate goroutine to avoid blocking the signal handler. The goroutine must loop and read from the signal channel until the process exits. Traces to srd002-sys R3.2.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"InstallSIGPIPEHandler must be safe to call multiple times. Subsequent calls are no-ops. Use sync.Once or equivalent to ensure the handler is installed exactly once. Traces to srd002-sys R3.3.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Use os/signal.Notify with a buffered channel (capacity 1) for both SIGWINCH and SIGPIPE handlers. Buffered channels prevent signal loss when the receiver is briefly busy.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"InstallSIGPIPEHandler exits with os.Exit(0) on SIGPIPE, matching GNU coreutils behavior. All cmd/ utilities call this at the start of main().\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"OnTerminalResize spawns a background goroutine. There is no mechanism to unregister -- the handler lives for the process lifetime, matching the typical use case (ls, pr).\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"InstallSIGPIPEHandler can be called multiple times without panic or duplicate handlers.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"OnTerminalResize accepts a callback and does not block the caller.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"OnTerminalResize invokes the callback with the current terminal width when SIGWINCH is received.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"go build ./pkg/sys/... and go vet ./pkg/sys/... succeed with no errors.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":60575,"cache_read_input_tokens":11311,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":60575},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b4cf42cf-03bc-4dd5-b31d-2653e75b6e4b","uuid":"c812c1c9-b98e-491b-a37c-674d84bdc374"}
